{"t":1794762564,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"85508746-561f-4f8f-bb1e-75ba466d1af4","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-15T13:40:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 151340\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n840 AM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nFine tuned timing and placement of sprinkles east of the \nAppalachians through tonight.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Colder than normal through Monday with gradually\n  diminishing winds and clouds. \n\n- 2) Dry conditions persist through the upcoming week with \n  increasing temperatures starting Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder than normal through Monday with gradually\ndiminishing winds and clouds.\n\nNorthwest flow off the Great Lakes continues. This is resulting\nin intermittent snow showers along and west of the Allegheny\nFront, with sprinkles possible to the east through this evening\nas multiple waves aloft pass through.\n\nOtherwise, it will be partly to mostly cloudy for most as\nstratocumulus pivots overhead. Northwest gusts of 15 to 30 mph\nwill gradually subside through Monday. Gusts will be slower to \nsubside at higher elevations (especially at night compared to \nthe lower elevations). Clouds will also gradually thin and \nscatter out during this period.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions persist through the upcoming \nweek with increasing temperatures starting Tuesday.\n\nWinds continue to diminish on Tuesday and remain light through \nthe end of the week as high pressure transitions overhead in the\nwake of the front. As upper-level ridging continues to build \nover several days, dry conditions are expected to persist \nthrough the end of the forecast period with mostly clear skies \noverhead and progressively warming temperatures as the week goes\non. Afternoon highs could begin to approach the upper 60s \nbetween Thursday and Friday, well above seasonable normals, with\novernight lows generally in the 40s. No precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated Tuesday through Friday.\n\nLooking ahead to the end of the week, an upper-level trough \ncould develop over the Great Lakes and produce a stemming cold \nfront in the days following; will continue to monitor this \npattern for any precipitation potential over the coming days.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nOther than a spotty sprinkle today, it will be dry for all \nterminals through the upcoming week. Wind gusts to around 20 \nknots are anticipated today, and some 10-15 knot gusts may even \nlinger into tonight. By Monday, winds taper off a little bit \nmore, before becoming much lighter by Monday night.\n\nNW winds stay below 10 knots Tuesday through Thursday as high \npressure remains overhead. VFR conditions are anticipated across \nterminals through this period, with mostly clear skies persisting \nthrough midweek. Winds shift southwesterly on Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNorth to northwest winds remain elevated today across all\nwaters, with gusts around 20 to 25 knots expected. SCAs remain\nin effect through this evening for all waters, and have been\nextended overnight in southern portions of the waters. Winds\nwill taper off into Monday, but SCAs may still need to be\nextended at least for a portion of the day before that occurs.\n\nWinds stay below SCA thresholds Monday night through Thursday, \nshifting from northwesterly to southwesterly by Wednesday. No \nprecipitation is anticipated during this period as high pressure\nremains overhead.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nIt will remain brisk today as cool high pressure builds towards\nthe region and a departing low remains well to our northeast. \nAside from some intermittent mountain snow showers along and \nwest of the Allegheny Front, dry conditions are expected for \nmost. Brisk winds will continue through Monday with waves of \nclouds, but the magnitude will be less than recent days.\n\nHigh pressure gradually builds overhead into the middle of the \nweek, then temperatures will begin to warm mid to late week. \nDuring this timeframe, while winds will diminish, RH values \nwill go down substantially. The dry air really builds in \nstarting Tuesday into Tuesday night, and looks to continue for \nseveral days thereafter. Overnight recoveries, especially on the\nridges, will likely be poor as well starting Tuesday night. \nExpect afternoon RH values to drop well into the 20s each day. \nThis will also be paired with seasonably warm temperatures, so \nthis will really act to dry out already very dry fuels.\n\nNot helping matters is that there is no precipitation expected \neast of the Appalachians through at least the end of the week. \nGiven the setup, will really be watching for any potential dry \nfrontal passages in the long range as the next days to really \nwatch out for.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EST this evening for ANZ530-\n     531-535-536-538-539.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EST Monday for ANZ532>534-537-\n     540>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF/CJL/SRT\nAVIATION...DHOF/CJL/SRT\nMARINE...DHOF/CJL/SRT\nFIRE WEATHER...CJL/DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794762436,"days":{"2026-11-15":{"hi":54,"lo":38,"pop":4,"short":"Scattered Rain Showers"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":58,"lo":36,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":62,"lo":37,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":66,"lo":37,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-19":{"hi":66,"lo":41,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-20":{"hi":68,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-21":{"hi":67,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794762000,"n":48,"ceil":[4600,4400,5600,5500,5500,5500,5500,5600,5500,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,nu